{
  "abstract": "We prove a scoped closure for a known 24-vertex, 122-edge, nine-chromatic biplanar source built from two disjoint copies of K6 joined to C5 and two shared roots. Add two adjacent vertices x and y, allow arbitrary additional edges and arbitrary ownership between the two planar layers, and assume at most one edge joins the two private cores. Every resulting biplanar graph is nine-colorable, and the four boundary vertices can be assigned distinct colors. The proof isolates the simultaneous coloring issue: a local core must realize compatible boundary colors while five residual color classes are matched across the two cores. An exact finite palette model enumerates all five optimal C5 partitions and all boundary-block assignments. Eleven compact Glucose4 refutations, independently checked by RUP-only DRAT-trim and LRAT, establish the local extension and forced-color premises under the necessary K9, pentagram, and Euler constraints. A connected-core contraction handles the only remaining case, when both cross-edge endpoints are forced to the same root color. The supplement contains the generator, semantic and symmetry audit, checker sources, proof hashes, and a clean replay. A literal 26-vertex, 141-edge biplanar control is included. The result leaves two or more cross edges, nonadjacent consumers, and the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem open.",
